Google Cloud OAuth 2.0 Integration & Setup Guide

Follow these steps in your Google Cloud Console to configure your OAuth 2.0 authentication for any environment.

1. Authorized JavaScript Origins & Redirect URIs Configuration

Google strictly blocks requests if the requesting website's origin or redirect endpoint is not pre-registered in your OAuth Client ID settings.
1. Open the Google Cloud Console.
2. Select your project from the project dropdown at the top of the page.
3. In the left-hand menu, navigate to APIs & Services > Credentials.
4. Under the OAuth 2.0 Client IDs section, click on your target Web client ID.
5. Scroll down to Authorized JavaScript origins:
	• Click + ADD URI.
	• Add your application's domain URLs (e.g., https://your-production-domain.com, https://your-staging-domain.com, or http://localhost:3000).
	• Note: Do not include a trailing slash (/) at the end of the URL.
6. Scroll down to Authorized redirect URIs:
	• Click + ADD URI.
	• Add your backend or frontend authentication callback endpoints (e.g., https://your-production-domain.com or http://localhost:3000).
7. Click Save at the bottom of the page.

2. OAuth Consent Screen "Test Users" List

If your OAuth consent screen publishing status is set to "Testing", Google automatically blocks any Google account that is not explicitly listed as an approved tester.
1. In the left-hand menu, go to APIs & Services > OAuth consent screen.
2. Scroll down to the Test users section.
3. Click + ADD USERS.
4. Enter the email addresses of the developers, QA testers, or stakeholders who need access during development.
5. Click Save / Add.

3. Google Worldwide Edge Propagation

• Replication Delay: When an OAuth Client ID is created or updated in the Google Cloud Console, it typically takes 3 to 10 minutes for changes to replicate globally across Google's edge authentication infrastructure. If you encounter an error immediately after updating settings, wait a few minutes and try again.

